readline: include <sys/ioctl.h> so ioctl(2) is always prototyped

readline only includes <sys/ioctl.h> when configure sets
GWINSZ_IN_SYS_IOCTL, i.e. when termios.h does not already provide
TIOCGWINSZ. On Darwin termios.h pulls in <sys/ttycom.h>, which defines
TIOCGWINSZ and struct winsize, so the include is skipped and every
ioctl() call in terminal.c, rltty.c, input.c and util.c compiles
against an implicit declaration (allowed by the
-Wno-error=implicit-function-declaration the build needs for this
2017-era tree).

That is fatal on arm64 macOS: ioctl(2) is variadic, and the Darwin
arm64 ABI passes variadic arguments on the stack while an implicitly
declared (non-variadic) call passes them in registers. libSystem's
ioctl therefore reads the destination pointer from an uninitialized
stack slot and the kernel writes the TIOCGWINSZ result (the terminal's
struct winsize) through it — in practice into a just-freed heap block,
which macOS malloc detects at the next allocation:

  malloc: Incorrect checksum for freed object 0x...: probably
  modified after being freed.

and gdb aborts at startup right after the banner (the corrupt value
decodes exactly to the terminal's winsize). x86-64 passes the first
few arguments identically for variadic and normal calls, which is why
the old Intel macOS builds of this same source never crashed; on Linux
GWINSZ_IN_SYS_IOCTL is set and the include happens anyway.

Include <sys/ioctl.h> unconditionally (except MinGW, which has no such
header) from rldefs.h, which all of the affected files include.

--- a/readline/rldefs.h
+++ b/readline/rldefs.h
@@ -30,6 +30,14 @@
 
 #include "rlstdc.h"
 
+/* Always pick up the ioctl(2) prototype: on Darwin the TIOCGWINSZ
+   macros come in via termios.h, so the GWINSZ_IN_SYS_IOCTL-guarded
+   includes elsewhere are skipped and ioctl would otherwise be called
+   without a prototype (miscompiled on arm64 Darwin: ioctl is variadic).  */
+#if !defined (__MINGW32__)
+#  include <sys/ioctl.h>
+#endif
+
 #if defined (STRCOLL_BROKEN)
 #  undef HAVE_STRCOLL
 #endif
